.TH NAUTHILUS-DIRECTOR.YAML 5 "2026-06-05" "nauthilus-director" "File Formats"
.SH NAME
nauthilus-director.yaml \- Nauthilus Director YAML configuration format
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B nauthilus-director.yaml
is the canonical name for the Director configuration file format.
Files ending in
.B .yml
are also accepted.
.PP
The default format is YAML.
The loader also supports common Viper formats where practical, but operator
documentation and examples use YAML.
.SH STABLE PATH GROUPS
.TP
.B runtime
Process identity, shutdown timing, client timeouts and listener groups.
.TP
.B runtime.servers.control
The in-process control API listener, control authentication and control TLS.
.TP
.B runtime.state
Bounded Redis runtime-state reaper timing, index sharding, pagination limits
and backend reservation repair settings.
.TP
.B observability
Logging, metrics, tracing and diagnostic profiles.
.TP
.B storage.redis
Central Redis connection, security, Cluster, Sentinel, namespace and health
settings for production runtime coordination.
.TP
.B auth.authorities
Nauthilus authentication authorities, HTTP and gRPC transport settings,
mechanism policy and protected caller credentials.
.TP
.B director.listeners
Public protocol listeners, TLS, PROXY protocol, service names, authorities and
backend-pool bindings.
.TP
.B director.routing
Selector defaults, hash inputs, default shard and failover policy.
.TP
.B director.affinity
Active user backend-node pinning, retained backend binding, affinity key
construction, release timing, failover and local cache behavior.
.TP
.B director.runtime_overrides
Runtime control permissions for backend and user operations.
.TP
.B director.health
Backend health timing, jitter and transition thresholds.
.TP
.B director.maintenance
Default maintenance mode, drain timing, hard-kill grace and audit behavior.
.TP
.B director.backend_pools
Backend pool protocol, selector and backend membership.
.TP
.B director.backends
Backend protocol, shard, backend-node identity, address, weight, max
connections, maintenance baseline, transport security, authentication and
health checks.
.SH CONTROL PLANE AUTHENTICATION
The control API listener under
.B runtime.servers.control
protects generated
.B /api/v1/*
routes by default.
When metrics are served by the same control process,
.B /metrics
uses the same authentication boundary.
.B /healthz
and
.B /readyz
remain unauthenticated liveness and readiness endpoints because they return
secret-safe process state only.
.PP
Static bearer authentication is configured with
.BR runtime.servers.control.auth.bearer .
Set
.B enabled: true
and provide
.B token_file
as a mounted secret file.
The token is compared in constant time and is never printed by server or CLI
diagnostics.
.PP
mTLS control authentication is configured with
.BR runtime.servers.control.auth.mtls .
It requires
.B runtime.servers.control.tls.enabled: true
and
.BR runtime.servers.control.tls.client_ca .
Only certificates verified by the TLS stack are accepted as authenticated
actors.
.PP
OIDC control authentication is configured with
.BR runtime.servers.control.auth.oidc .
The configured
.B authority
must reference an enabled Nauthilus authority with OIDC client credentials.
Incoming bearer tokens are introspected through Nauthilus; inactive tokens,
tokens without a matching
.B required_audience
or
.BR required_resource ,
missing required scopes and malformed bearer input fail closed.
.B required_scopes
authorize ordinary control reads and mutations.
.B protected_scopes
are additionally required for protected configuration output.
.PP
Unauthorized requests return
.B 401
with generic problem details.
Authenticated actors without sufficient scope return
.BR 403 .
Protected config dumps and mutation commands fail as a whole when unauthorized;
partial protected output is not returned.
.SH PROTECTED DIAGNOSTIC PROFILES
Runtime profiles are disabled by default.
The master switch is
.BR observability.profiles.pprof.enabled .
When false, requests under
.B /debug/pprof/
return
.B 404
and behave like absent routes.
.PP
When pprof is enabled, profile endpoints are registered only on the
.B runtime.servers.control
listener. They are not registered on IMAP, LMTP, ManageSieve or POP3
listeners and are not exposed through a separate debug process or port.
Every pprof route uses the same control-plane authentication boundary as
.B /api/v1/*
and
.BR /metrics ,
then requires protected authorization. Ordinary static bearer or mTLS control
authentication does not grant protected profile access by itself.
OIDC callers need both
.B required_scopes
and a configured
.B protected_scopes
value.
.PP
The goroutine, block and mutex profile classes are independently explicit:
.BR observability.profiles.goroutine.enabled ,
.B observability.profiles.block.enabled
and
.BR observability.profiles.mutex.enabled .
Each requires
.B observability.profiles.pprof.enabled: true
and defaults to
.BR false .
Block and mutex sampling add process-wide runtime overhead and should be
enabled only for a bounded incident window.
.PP
Profiles can include goroutine stacks, allocation paths, command-line
arguments, timing data and contention information. Treat profile payloads as
sensitive diagnostic artifacts. Store them outside long-lived logs, avoid
sharing them broadly and delete or expire collected files after the incident or
performance investigation is complete.
.PP
Diagnostic access emits a bounded audit event with route class, result,
auth method and actor context. It does not log profile bodies, bearer tokens,
private keys, protected config values or raw certificates, and profile route
metrics use only low-cardinality labels.
.SH NAUTHILUS AUTHORITY CALLER AUTH
Director-to-Nauthilus caller authentication is configured under
.BR auth.authorities.<name> .
The
.B oidc.client_credentials
subtree enables Nauthilus-issued client-credentials access tokens for authority
calls.
The token endpoint is resolved from the configured
.B issuer
or
.BR discovery_url ,
and the discovery document must advertise the configured token endpoint client
authentication method.
.PP
The initial supported token endpoint authentication methods are
.B client_secret_basic
,
.B client_secret_post
and
.BR private_key_jwt .
Client secrets are protected config values. Operators may provide
.B client_secret
directly through protected config inspection paths or reference mounted secret
material with
.BR client_secret_file .
Both values are redacted by default in generated config dumps and REST config
readbacks.
.PP
For
.BR private_key_jwt ,
configure
.B token_endpoint_auth_method: private_key_jwt
with
.BR client_private_key_file .
Optional
.BR client_key_id ,
.B client_assertion_alg
and
.B audience
values control the JWT header and audience claim.
Supported assertion algorithms are
.B RS256
and
.BR EdDSA .
The same authority may use
.B introspection_endpoint_auth_method
with
.BR client_secret_basic ,
.B client_secret_post
or
.B private_key_jwt
for control-plane OIDC introspection, subject to the methods announced by the
Nauthilus discovery document.
This field is not used for mail SASL bearer-token introspection.
.PP
When HTTP authority transport is selected and
.B oidc.client_credentials.enabled
is true, authority requests send
.B Authorization: Bearer
caller tokens and do not also send Basic authentication.
Disabling OIDC client credentials keeps the explicit
.B http.basic_auth
compatibility path available.
.PP
For gRPC authority transport, enable
.B grpc.caller_auth.oidc.enabled
to send
.B authorization: Bearer
metadata. The
.B grpc.caller_auth.basic
and
.B grpc.caller_auth.bearer
subtrees remain explicit compatibility or emergency modes, but only one gRPC
caller-auth method may be enabled at a time.
.PP
These caller tokens are separate from mail-protocol
.B XOAUTH2
and
.B OAUTHBEARER
credentials. The static policy for mail SASL bearer validation lives under
.BR auth.authorities.<name>.mechanisms.bearer.introspection ,
not under
.BR oidc.client_credentials .
The
.B validation
value for bearer-capable mechanisms is
.BR nauthilus_introspection .
.PP
The introspection subtree configures issuer or discovery URL, endpoint
client-auth material, the required end-user token audience or resource, the
required end-user token scope and an optional account claim. A direct discovery
URL requires a pinned issuer and HTTPS outside loopback. The default
.B required_scope
is
.BR email .
Inline client secrets, secret files and private-key file paths are protected
config values. Supported endpoint auth methods are
.BR client_secret_basic ,
.B client_secret_post
and
.BR private_key_jwt .
Director does not locally validate, log or cache end-user mail bearer tokens.
.PP
Operational setup and migration guidance for Nauthilus OIDC caller auth lives
in
.BR docs/operations/oidc-nauthilus.md .
.SH LISTENER AUTHORITY CONTEXT
Protocol listeners may define
.B director.listeners.<name>.authority_context.http_headers
and
.B director.listeners.<name>.authority_context.grpc_metadata
as static, listener-owned deployment facts for the selected Nauthilus
authority.
HTTP context entries are added to outbound Nauthilus HTTP authority requests as
headers. gRPC context entries are added as outgoing metadata for
authentication, identity lookup and account-listing calls.
.PP
These values are not routing hints, backend selectors, tenant routing input,
user identity, dynamic claims or secrets. They must not contain credentials,
tokens, session identifiers or private deployment data.
Nauthilus consumes them only when its own policy configuration explicitly
allowlists the names through
.B auth.policy.request_headers
or
.BR auth.policy.request_metadata .
.PP
Director validates names and values during config loading. Empty values,
credential headers such as
.B Authorization
or
.BR Cookie ,
transport-owned headers such as
.B Content-Type
and gRPC-reserved
.B grpc-*
metadata names fail closed. HTTP header names are canonicalized; gRPC metadata
keys must already be lowercase ASCII metadata keys.
.SH BACKEND OUTBOUND PROXY PROTOCOL
Backend entries may enable backend-side HAProxy PROXY protocol with
.B director.backends.<name>.haproxy.enabled: true.
When enabled, every Director-created TCP connection to that backend writes one
PROXY preface before backend TLS, STARTTLS negotiation, greeting reads,
capability discovery, authentication or protocol forwarding.
The setting applies to real IMAP and LMTP sessions and to light and deep backend
health checks.
.PP
When
.B haproxy.enabled
is false, no outbound PROXY preface is written.
There is no implemented
.B backend PROXY protocol version
configuration path; generated references and examples intentionally show only
.BR enabled .
.PP
.B backend_node
is the concrete mailbox-authority identity used for placement, active affinity
and retained backend binding. It is not a proxy hostname, socket address,
HAProxy endpoint name or transport selector.
Backend-side PROXY handling does not change routing, health ownership,
maintenance, drain, runtime weight or YAML configuration.
.SH RUNTIME STATE AND DEVELOPMENT RESET
Redis runtime keys use
.B storage.redis.schema_version
inside the configured key prefix.
During the current development-stage runtime-state redesign, the default schema
version remains
.BR 1 .
The v1 key layout is being updated in place and is not yet a published
production storage compatibility contract.
.PP
Scale knobs live under
.BR runtime.state .
.B runtime.state.reaper
controls bounded expired-session and reservation repair passes.
.B runtime.state.indexes
sets repairable index shard counts and REST/CLI page bounds.
.B runtime.state.backend_reservations
sets backend capacity reservation lease and repair timing.
These settings tune runtime behavior; Redis topology, authentication and TLS
remain centralized under
.BR storage.redis .
.PP
Old demo or development runtime keys may be allowed to expire according to
their leases, or cleared explicitly after stopping Director processes that use
the same Redis database.
Clearing old runtime keys is an operator action for demo and non-production
environments only.
Do not silently delete active production sessions and do not claim production
compatibility for old v1 key layouts without a separate breaking-change or
migration decision.
.SH LMTP LISTENERS AND BACKENDS
LMTP listener entries use
.B director.listeners.<name>.protocol: lmtp
and bind to an LMTP backend pool with
.BR backend_pool .
Plain LMTP listeners normally use
.B tls.mode: starttls
when STARTTLS is offered.
LMTPS-style listeners use
.B tls.mode: implicit
and still use
.B protocol: lmtp
with an operator-facing service name such as
.BR lmtps .
.PP
Supported listener-specific LMTP settings live under
.BR director.listeners.<name>.lmtp .
The implemented surface uses configured
.B capabilities
for the
.B LHLO
reply, including SMTPUTF8 and ENHANCEDSTATUSCODES, and peer authentication
through
.BR client_auth .
The configured capability list is a desired surface, not a blind backend mirror:
CHUNKING and 8BITMIME are advertised only when the Director implements the
frontend behavior and fresh backend-pool health proof shows every backend member
supports the matching extension.
The configured
.B capabilities
list is a positive frontend allowlist: omitted LMTP extensions are not
advertised and their frontend parameters or commands fail closed.
.B capability_filter.deny
is a listener-scoped hard deny for whole capability names. Denied capabilities
are not advertised and are not consumed from backend-pool or selected-backend
capability proof for that listener. Disable frontend
.B BDAT
and backend BDAT delivery optimization through
.B CHUNKING
rather than by listing command names.
.B size.max_message_bytes
is the listener-owned fixed LMTP message-size policy. A positive value is
required before
.B SIZE
can be configured and advertised. Runtime
.B LHLO
advertises
.B SIZE
with the effective maximum only when listener policy permits it and every
eligible backend has fresh
.B SIZE
proof; the effective maximum is the lower of the listener value and any
backend-pool maximum. If
.B SIZE
is omitted, denied or not advertised in the current session,
.B MAIL FROM
with
.B SIZE=<n>
fails closed. Accepted declarations are forwarded on backend
.B MAIL FROM
only to selected backends that advertised
.BR SIZE ,
and the Director does not synthesize a backend
.B SIZE=
parameter when the frontend omitted one.
.B DATA
and
.B BDAT
bodies are counted against the same effective maximum and permanent size
failures stop backend delivery without queueing, retry scheduling, bounces or
message spooling.
.B PIPELINING
is a frontend ordered-reply capability. It permits clients to group frontend
commands, but it does not make the Director batch
.BR MAIL ,
.BR RCPT ,
.B DATA
or
.B BDAT
commands to the selected backend.
Peer authentication verifies the submitting LMTP peer; it is separate from
recipient routing and does not authenticate the mailbox recipient.
.PP
LMTP backend entries use
.B director.backends.<name>.protocol: lmtp
and should share
.B shard_tag
and
.B backend_node
values with other protocol backends that represent the same concrete mailstore
authority.
Backend TLS, SASL, OAuth bearer, credential replay, master-user and health-check
settings use the shared backend config paths.
Supported LMTP backend auth modes are
.BR none ,
.BR mtls ,
.B sasl
and
.BR oauthbearer .
.B auth.mode: none
sends no backend credentials and is the normal plaintext backend choice on
trusted internal delivery networks when
.B tls.mode
is
.BR plaintext ,
.B disabled
or
.BR none .
This is not the general recommended default.
Credential-bearing SASL and OAuth bearer backend auth keep
.B require_tls: true
by default; with that policy they require verified backend TLS and reject
plaintext backend TLS modes.
Setting
.B require_tls: false
is an explicit weaker policy that permits configured service credentials on the
backend hop.
.B mtls
always requires verified backend TLS plus configured
.B tls.cert
and
.BR tls.key .
LMTP health checks use the same backend TLS and auth policy as real LMTP
sessions, then stop before envelope or message-body commands.
Frontend
.B CHUNKING
advertisement is listener policy and is required before accepting frontend
.B BDAT .
Backend
.B CHUNKING
usage is selected separately from the chosen backend's
.B LHLO
capabilities. A CHUNKING-capable backend may receive backend
.B BDAT
for either frontend
.B DATA
or frontend
.B BDAT
transactions. When the selected backend does not advertise
.BR CHUNKING ,
frontend
.B DATA
falls back to backend
.B DATA
and frontend
.B BDAT
fails closed without backend
.B BDAT
writes.
BODY=8BITMIME is accepted only when 8BITMIME was advertised in the current
LHLO response and is forwarded to the selected backend without inspecting,
rewriting or downgrading DATA or BDAT payload bytes.
After a successful delivery or transaction close, LMTP retains the selected
backend node under
.B director.affinity.backend_retention
for
.B 15m
by default so immediate mailbox access does not move to another backend node
while the backend finishes index or storage synchronization.
.PP
LMTP route lookup through
.BR nauthilus-directorctl (1)
accepts
.B --recipient
for recipient diagnostics.
It is a read-only route explanation; it may use the configured no-auth
Nauthilus identity lookup path, but it does not authenticate credentials, create
sessions, open backend connections or mutate Redis state.
.SH MANAGESIEVE LISTENERS AND BACKENDS
ManageSieve listener entries use the canonical config protocol value
.BR sieve .
Operator-facing listener names may be
.B sieve
for STARTTLS and
.B sieves
for implicit TLS, but backend pools, route lookup, runtime state and metrics
still use
.BR protocol:\ sieve .
.PP
Supported listener-specific settings live under
.BR director.listeners.<name>.sieve .
.B auth_mechanisms
is typed SASL mechanism policy and supports
.BR plain ,
.B xoauth2
and
.BR oauthbearer
when the selected Nauthilus authority enables the corresponding mechanism
class.
Credential-bearing SASL requires frontend TLS gating; listeners must use
.B tls.mode: starttls
or
.BR tls.mode:\ implicit .
.PP
ManageSieve capability settings are typed facts, not raw protocol transcript
lines.
.B capabilities.script_extensions
is the operator-declared common backend-pool Sieve extension set that may be
advertised before a user backend is selected.
.B capabilities.language
is a safe language tag for response text.
RFC 5804
.B IMPLEMENTATION
and
.B VERSION
facts are not operator-configurable; the protocol handler derives
.B IMPLEMENTATION
from the binary version and uses the RFC 5804 protocol version
.BR 1.0 .
RFC 5804 wire rendering belongs to the protocol handler and must also account
for listener TLS state, configured mechanisms and backend transport safety.
.PP
ManageSieve backend pools use
.B director.backend_pools.<name>.protocol: sieve
with the
.B rendezvous_hash
selector.
Backend entries use
.B director.backends.<name>.protocol: sieve
and should share
.B shard_tag
and
.B backend_node
values with sibling protocol endpoints for the same concrete mailstore.
Supported backend auth modes are
.B master_user
and
.BR credential_replay .
.B credential_replay
requires verified backend TLS and an explicit allowed-mechanism list.
.PP
Route lookup accepts
.B --protocol sieve
with a caller-supplied user key for diagnostics.
It is read-only: it does not authenticate credentials, create sessions, connect
to ManageSieve backends or inspect script names or script contents.
.SH POP3 LISTENERS AND BACKENDS
POP3 listener entries use the canonical config protocol value
.BR pop3 .
Operator-facing listener names may be
.B pop3
for STLS-capable cleartext startup and
.B pop3s
for implicit TLS, but backend pools, route lookup, runtime state and metrics
still use
.BR protocol:\ pop3 .
.PP
Supported listener-specific settings live under
.BR director.listeners.<name>.pop3 .
.B auth_mechanisms
is typed frontend method policy.
.B userpass
controls the POP3
.B USER
and
.B PASS
flow, while
.B xoauth2
and
.B oauthbearer
control the matching POP3
.B AUTH
mechanisms when the selected Nauthilus authority enables the corresponding
class.
Credential-bearing methods require frontend TLS gating; listeners must use
.B tls.mode: starttls
or
.BR tls.mode:\ implicit .
.PP
POP3
.B capabilities
are bounded CAPA policy tokens, not raw protocol transcript lines.
The protocol handler derives any SASL CAPA mechanism text from
.B auth_mechanisms
and must account for listener TLS state before advertising or accepting
credential-bearing commands.
The
.B pop3s
service name is never a protocol value.
.PP
POP3 backend pools use
.B director.backend_pools.<name>.protocol: pop3
with the
.B rendezvous_hash
selector.
Backend entries use
.B director.backends.<name>.protocol: pop3
and should share
.B shard_tag
and
.B backend_node
values with sibling protocol endpoints for the same concrete mailstore.
Supported backend auth modes are
.B master_user
and
.BR credential_replay .
.B credential_replay
requires verified backend TLS and an explicit allowed-mechanism list.
.SH OBSERVABILITY SAFETY
LMTP, ManageSieve and POP3 logs, traces and Prometheus metrics expose operation,
result, bounded
reason class, backend pool, shard tag, mechanism, transport, TLS mode and status
class where applicable.
ManageSieve and POP3 observations additionally include the pre-auth phase spans
.B nauthilus_director.sieve.pre_auth
and
.BR nauthilus_director.pop3.pre_auth ,
then reuse the shared session, Nauthilus auth, routing, backend select, backend
connect and proxy pipe trace boundaries.
Backend-side PROXY observations use the operation
.B backend_proxy_protocol
and bounded reason classes such as
.BR ok ,
.BR disabled ,
.BR write_failed ,
.BR missing_address ,
.B unsupported_family
and
.BR config .
Metric labels remain low-cardinality and do not include usernames, recipients,
client addresses, session IDs, backend identifiers, backend nodes, raw errors,
raw frontend or backend socket addresses, or secrets.
Backend identifiers and backend nodes may appear only in logs and traces where
the operator-diagnostic policy permits them; they are never Prometheus labels.
.PP
Observable LMTP output must not contain raw recipients, envelope senders,
message IDs, subjects, DATA or BDAT content. Observable ManageSieve output must
not contain raw usernames, script names, script contents, post-auth command
bodies, client addresses or session IDs. Observable POP3 output must not
contain raw usernames, mailbox command payloads, message numbers, UIDLs, message
sizes, message contents, client addresses or session IDs. These protocol paths
also keep peer passwords, bearer tokens, SASL blobs, private keys and raw
authorization headers out of logs, traces, metrics and test failure output.
Operators should use status classes and reason classes to distinguish POP3
auth, routing, hold, backend-pin, backend and proxy failures, plus LMTP DATA and
BDAT failures, without exposing message or script content.
.SH INCLUDES AND PATCHES
Loader-only keys
.BR includes ,
.B env
and
.B patch
are processed before typed validation and are not present in the final runtime
snapshot or config dump output.
.PP
.B includes
supports required, optional and environment-specific include lists.
Relative include paths are resolved from the declaring file directory.
Recursive include cycles fail closed.
Missing required includes fail loading; missing optional includes are ignored.
.PP
.B patch
is a list of operations with
.BR op ,
.B path
and
.B value .
Supported operations are
.BR add ,
.B replace
and
.BR remove .
Patches from included files are collected before patches from the including
file and are applied before placeholder expansion.
.SH PLACEHOLDERS
Scalar values may use
.B ${NAME}
placeholders.
Expansion happens before mapstructure decoding and typed validation.
Map keys are never expanded.
Missing variables fail closed.
Ordinary dollar signs remain literal, and
.B $${NAME}
escapes a placeholder.
.SH ENVIRONMENT OVERRIDES
Stable scalar config paths can be overridden with
.B NAUTHILUS_DIRECTOR_
environment variables derived from the config path.
For example,
.B runtime.servers.control.address
maps to
.BR NAUTHILUS_DIRECTOR_RUNTIME_SERVERS_CONTROL_ADDRESS .
Overrides are applied after placeholder expansion and before typed validation.
.SH RELOAD AND RESTART SCOPE
Safe reload validates the mounted configuration again and applies only config
classes with live owners in the running process.
The reloadable classes are listener additions/removals,
.BR director.backends.* ,
and
.BR director.backend_pools.* .
Existing listener definitions are not hot-swapped in place.
.PP
Restart is required for
.BR runtime.* ,
.BR auth.* ,
.BR storage.redis.* ,
.BR observability.* ,
.BR director.routing ,
.BR director.affinity ,
.BR director.runtime_overrides ,
.BR director.health ,
.BR director.maintenance ,
.BR director.security ,
and edits to existing
.B director.listeners.*
entries.
A rejected reload leaves the previously accepted runtime snapshot active and
does not rewrite YAML.
Existing sessions, active affinities, backend pins and placement holds are
preserved unless a separate explicit runtime command changes them.
.SH REDACTION AND PROTECTED VALUES
Passwords, token files, private keys and other secret-bearing fields are marked
as protected in the typed config model.
Config dump output is redacted by default.
Local
.B nauthilus-director config dump -P
and remote
.B nauthilus-directorctl config dump -P
are the explicit protected-value paths.
Remote protected output additionally requires the Director to authorize the
protected config request; a
.B 403
response means no protected output is printed.
.SH OPERATIONS DOCUMENTATION
Deployment, security, OIDC, reload, failure-mode and migration runbooks are
maintained under
.BR docs/operations/ .
Those documents use placeholder or file-path examples for credentials and must
not contain literal passwords, bearer tokens or private keys.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR nauthilus-director (1),
.BR nauthilus-directorctl (1)
